  "result": "{\"seen_before\": true, \"matches\": [\"terminal#16330\", \"terminal#19839\", \"terminal#16024\"], \"answer\": \"This has been reported before. A starting directory containing square brackets, whether passed via wt -d / --startingDirectory or via Explorer's 'Open in Terminal', is a known limitation of Windows PowerShell 5.1. It cannot be launched with a working directory containing '[', so it falls back to its own directory (or C:\\\\). The Terminal team treats it as a duplicate of terminal#11149 and says Terminal cannot work around it. Past tickets report that the fix is to use PowerShell 7+ (pwsh) as the profile, which handles the path correctly. Set PowerShell 7 as your default profile, or use Command Prompt, which is not affected. Note that your own check shows Set-Location -LiteralPath works, but that cannot be applied to the way Terminal launches the shell.\"}",
